@section('structured_data')<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebPage","name":"Context and composers","url":"https://propel.rocketboost.rs/docs/context","description":"Each Propel view has one Rust context struct, filled by its handler. The view reads the struct's fields by name and cannot use a field it lacks."}</script>@endsection

@section('title')Context and composers@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>Context and composers</h1>
  <p>Every view has one context: a Rust struct that holds the data the view needs. The handler builds it, and the view reads its fields. A view cannot use a field that the struct does not have.</p>

  <h2>Define the context</h2>
  <p>Derive <code>Serialize</code> so Propel can read the fields. A context also has <code>nav</code> and <code>pager</code> fields when its layout reads them, as every context on this site does. <code>Group</code> and <code>Pager</code> are in <code>src/nav.rs</code>:</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">#[derive(Serialize)]
pub struct PostsIndex {
    pub title: String,
    pub posts: Vec&lt;Post&gt;,
    pub nav: Vec&lt;Group&gt;,
    pub pager: Pager,
}</code></pre>
  <p>To use a struct in <code>@@if</code>, derive <code>Truthy</code> from <code>propel_macros</code> as well: <code>#[derive(Serialize, Truthy)]</code>. A derived struct is true. A struct without the derive fails the build.</p>

  <h2>Build it in a handler</h2>
  <p>The route is the view composer for this page: it collects the data, fills <code>nav</code> and <code>pager</code> with <code>Chrome::at</code>, and passes the context to the compiled view:</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">#[get("/posts")]
fn posts() -&gt; PropelView {
    let chrome = Chrome::at("/posts");
    PropelView::from_result(compiled::posts::index::render(
        &amp;PostsIndex {
            title: "Posts".into(),
            posts: all_posts(),
            nav: chrome.nav,
            pager: chrome.pager,
        },
        &amp;Assets::new("public"),
    ))
}</code></pre>
  <p>A debug build can call <code>PropelView::render("posts.index", &amp;ctx)</code> with the same context. It reads the view from <code>resources/views</code> on each request.</p>

  <h2>Read it in the view</h2>
  <p>Fields are read by name, as in <code>$title</code> or <code>$post.title</code>. The <code>$</code> is optional, and a loop variable such as <code>post</code> is read the same way.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@extends('layouts.app')

@@section('content')
  &lt;h1&gt;@{{ $title }}&lt;/h1&gt;
  @@forelse (post in posts)
    &lt;h2&gt;@{{ $post.title }}&lt;/h2&gt;
  @@empty
    &lt;p&gt;No posts yet.&lt;/p&gt;
  @@endforelse
@@endsection</code></pre>

  <h2>Shared fields</h2>
  <p>The sidebar and the previous and next links are needed on every page. This site fills them in each handler, with <code>Chrome::at</code> from <code>src/nav.rs</code>, and the layout reads them like any other field:</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@foreach (group in nav)
  &lt;p&gt;@{{ $group.heading }}&lt;/p&gt;
  @@foreach (link in group.links)
    &lt;a href="@{{ $link.href }}"&gt;@{{ $link.label }}&lt;/a&gt;
  @@endforeach
@@endforeach</code></pre>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <p>Propel has no shared-data wrapper yet. Each context lists its shared fields itself, because compiled views read the fields by name. A <code>#[serde(flatten)]</code> wrapper works in the dev renderer only. A view composer that runs on every request is planned, but not built. <code>Chrome</code> is part of this site, not of the library.</p>
@endsection
